ical reagent - it is the 'separately prepared BP-degrading enzyme' that digests residual PBSA so it can be quantified as succinic acid - but the experiment is nonetheless a direct assay of a purified recombinant enzyme against the polymer, with monomer release measured, so it is recorded as an interaction in its own right. Source strain Pseudozyma antarctica JCM 10317 (now Moesziomyces antarcticus); expressed in E. coli Origami 2 (DE3) from pET28(+) carrying the mature PaE coding region (residues 27-224), induced with 1 mM IPTG at 24 C for 16 h, purified on HisTrap HP with the N-terminal His-tag then removed by thrombin cleavage and gel filtration, and further purified by affinity chromatography on emulsified PBSA. The authors state the recombinant enzyme had no significant difference in emulsified-PBSA-degrading activity from native PaE (data not shown).\",\"strain_occurrence\":null,\"paper_id\":197,\"paper\":{\"id\":197,\"citation\":\"Sameshima-Yamashita, Y., Koitabashi, M., Tsuchiya, W., Suzuki, K., Watanabe, T., Shinozaki, Y., Yamamoto-Tamura, K., Yamazaki, T., & Kitamoto, H. (2016). Enhancement of biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme production from Paraphoma-like fungus, strain B47-9. Journal of Oleo Science, 65(3), 257-262.\",\"title\":\"Enhancement of Biodegradable Plastic-degrading Enzyme Production from &lt;i&gt;Paraphoma&lt;/i&gt;-like Fungus, Strain B47-9\",\"doi\":\"10.5650/jos.ess15207\",\"year\":2016,\"authors\":\"Sameshima-Yamashita, Y.; Koitabashi, M.; Tsuchiya, W.; Suzuki, K.; Watanabe, T.; Shinozaki, Y.; Yamamoto-Tamura, K.; Yamazaki, T.; Kitamoto, H.\",\"journal\":\"Journal of Oleo Science\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":4549,\"method\":\"enzyme_assay\",\"temperature\":35,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":8,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":2,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"emulsion\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":false,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":0.42,\"unit\":\"mg/mL\",\"text_description\":\"0.90% (w/v) emulsified PBSA: about 0.42 mg/mL succinic acid released after 2 h with 0.17 uM PaE - the highest point of Fig. 3a.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":0.39,\"unit\":\"mg/mL\",\"text_description\":\"0.72% (w/v) emulsified PBSA: about 0.39 mg/mL succinic acid released (Fig. 3a).\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":0.31,\"unit\":\"mg/mL\",\"text_description\":\"0.45% (w/v) emulsified PBSA: about 0.31 mg/mL succinic acid released (Fig. 3a).\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":null},{\"id\":6832,\"plastic\":{\"id\":48,\"name\":\"PBSA\",\"abbreviation\":\"PBSA\"},\"actor_type\":\"protein\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"Purified PaE measurably clears an emulsified PBSA suspension, and this paper determines its specific activity against that substrate (13.41 +/- 1.30 U/mg). The enzyme is produced by P. antarctica itself throughout this work - the entire point of the paper is a self-cloning strain carrying no foreign DNA, and E. coli DH5-alpha is used only to propagate the cloning plasmid - so enzyme_production_context is 'native'. The paper does not, however, state which strain or purification the purified PaE standard came from; see reviewer_guidance. Evidence note: this is a turbidimetric assay on a polymer dispersion, so it shows the suspended PBSA particles disappearing rather than mass loss from a PBSA article. The paper's further claims that PaE degrades PBS and BP mulch films come from earlier studies and are not recorded here.\",\"strain_occurrence\":null,\"paper_id\":277,\"paper\":{\"id\":277,\"citation\":\"Sameshima-Yamashita, Y., Watanabe, T., Tanaka, T., Tsuboi, S., Yarimizu, T., Morita, T., Koike, H., Suzuki, K., & Kitamoto, H. (2019). Construction of a Pseudozyma antarctica strain without foreign DNA sequences (self-cloning strain) for high yield production of a biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme. Bioscience, Biotechnology, and Biochemistry, 83(8), 1547-1556.\",\"title\":\"Construction of a Pseudozyma antarctica strain without foreign DNA...\",\"doi\":\"10.1080/09168451.2019.1571898\",\"year\":2019,\"authors\":\"Sameshima-Yamashita, Y.; Watanabe, T.; Tanaka, T.; Tsuboi, S.; Yarimizu, T.; Morita, T.; Koike, H.; Suzuki, K.; Kitamoto, H.\",\"journal\":\"Bioscience, Biotechnology, and Biochemistry\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":15876,\"method\":\"turbidity\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.3,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":15,\"duration_unit\":\"minutes\",\"substrate_form\":\"emulsion\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":13.41,\"unit\":\"U/mg\",\"text_description\":\"Purified PaE degraded emulsified PBSA with a specific activity of 13.41 +/- 1.30 U/mg at 30 degC in 50 mM HEPES pH 7.3, measured as the fall in turbidity of a 0.05% (w/v) PBSA emulsion over 15 min.\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":null},{\"id\":8392,\"plastic\":{\"id\":48,\"name\":\"PBSA\",\"abbreviation\":\"PBSA\"},\"actor_type\":\"protein\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"PBSA is the paper's primary substrate for PaE. Purified PaE degraded emulsified PBSA with a specific activity of 54.8 +/- 6.3 U/mg, completely dissolved 20-micrometre commercial PBSA agricultural mulch film at 3.5-7 microM enzyme in 24 h, and solubilised 63.9% of a PBSA cast film to water-soluble carbon in 24 h. The enzyme was purified from the native producer Pseudozyma antarctica JCM 10317 (current NCBI name Moesziomyces antarcticus), so the interaction is typed native/purified; note that one experiment listed here (functional complementation) instead used the enzyme heterologously expressed in Saccharomyces cerevisiae BY4741 as an unpurified culture broth, which is stated in that experiment's notes.\",\"strain_occurrence\":null,\"paper_id\":331,\"paper\":{\"id\":331,\"citation\":\"Shinozaki, Y., Morita, T., Cao, X.-H., Yoshida, S., Koitabashi, M., Watanabe, T., Suzuki, K., Sameshima-Yamashita, Y., Nakajima-Kambe, T., Fujii, T., & Kitamoto, H. K. (2013). Biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme from Pseudozyma antarctica: cloning, sequencing, and characterization. Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology, 97(7), 2951-2959.\",\"title\":\"Biodegradable plastic-degrading enzyme from Pseudozyma antarctica:...\",\"doi\":\"10.1007/s00253-012-4188-8\",\"year\":2013,\"authors\":\"Shinozaki, Y.; Morita, T.; Cao, X.-H.; Yoshida, S.; Koitabashi, M.; Watanabe, T.; Suzuki, K.; Sameshima-Yamashita, Y.; Nakajima-Kambe, T.; Fujii, T.; Kitamoto, H.K.\",\"journal\":\"Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":19608,\"method\":\"turbidity